# Options for the JVM that `enso --jvm` starts (engine/runner JvmOptions.java), one per line,
# after the launcher's fixed list and before ENSO_JVM_OPTIONS. They are the engine's: the IDE
# and the CLI both get them. The IDE's per-user AOT cache key includes this file's text, so a
# change here retrains it (project-manager-shim jvmAot/key.ts).
#
# JDK 25 warns about these modules' restricted method calls; a later JDK makes them errors.
--enable-native-access=ALL-UNNAMED,org.enso.language.server

# Let C2 compile the engine's own Java, not Graal-as-JIT (JVMCI). Truffle guest code still
# compiles through libgraal. Opens a project faster: 0.63 s at 4 CPUs, 0.15 s at 32 (open-time
# measurements, #518). The CI peak guard (std-libs-benchmark, Table|Vector, runs 42435500911 and
# 42435507266) showed no systematic regression (median -1%). Three sorting rechecks (runs
# 42487383592, 42487394178, 42487404756) found no benchmark over 25% worse in all three
# (table_sort_ints: +32%, +48%, -1%). The text of this file is part of the IDE's AOT cache key,
# so the cache retrains.
-XX:-UseJVMCICompiler
